{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Aharon saw.</b> He saw many signs, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: what did he see? He saw Hur, son of Miriam, whom they had already killed. <b>And he built an altar before it.</b> He considered it from the offering before it. But Aharon was concerned that if he delivered himself to be killed, all Israel would be liable to utter destruction, in the manner of what is said (Lamentations 2:20): ״אם יהרג במקדש ה' כהן ונביא״ — and he wanted the sin to be hung on him and not on them. And further, he saw that if they build the altar — this one brings a stone and that one brings a clod — it all becomes as one, and since I am building it they will slacken in it and Moshe will come in the meantime. And further, we can say that he saw that the act is not included under 'be killed and do not transgress,' but rather under the negative prohibition of ״לא תעשה לך פסל״, and therefore he did not deliver himself over it."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The verse citation is given as איכה ב׳:כ׳-כ״א; the phrase quoted (אם יהרג במקדש ה') appears at Lamentations 2:20, and the range 2:20–21 is noted."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:11.948397809Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}